{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"florid-cookable-fly","creation_channel":"cli","description":"Discovered by the plan-review gate while re-reviewing mirror F3 (contiguous-industrial-dugong), which consolidated the claim-time plan-review exemption into rebar.types.PLAN_REVIEW_EXEMPT_TYPES.\n\n## Problem\n\nsrc/rebar/llm/plan_review/workflow_ops.py:108 carries a hardcoded tuple:\n\n    if pctx.ticket_type in (\"session_log\", \"code_review\", \"identity\"):\n\nIt short-circuits those types to a bare exempt PASS with no review run. The module imports nothing from rebar.types — verified by grep, no EXEMPT reference at all — so this is a seventh spelling list of the ticket-type vocabulary that nothing pins.\n\n## Why F3 did not cover it, and why this is still a mirror\n\nIt is NOT a duplicate of PLAN_REVIEW_EXEMPT_TYPES. That set is {bug, session_log, code_review, identity}; this one is the same set MINUS bug, because a bug no longer short-circuits — since epic 6982/R4 it gets a LIGHT ADVISORY review (DET floor + necessity probe). So the two answer different questions: \"is this type exempt from the claim gate?\" versus \"does this type skip review entirely?\". F3 correctly consolidated the first and left the second alone.\n\nIt is still a mirror worth closing: the three spellings must be TicketType members and nothing enforces it, so renaming code_review silently turns its short-circuit off and the type would start getting a full review with nothing failing — the same failure shape as the other mirrors in this epic.\n\n## Approach — derive the subset\n\nName the bare-exempt set in rebar.types, derived from PLAN_REVIEW_EXEMPT_TYPES rather than re-listed (it is the exempt set minus the types that take the bug review tier), and have workflow_ops consume it. Deriving is what keeps the two sets from drifting apart independently.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] AC1: workflow_ops.py:108 consumes a derived constant; no ticket-type spelling remains in that module. Verified by a test and by grep for the literals.\n- [ ] AC2: membership is UNCHANGED — exactly session_log, code_review and identity short-circuit, and a bug still takes the light advisory tier rather than a bare exempt PASS. Verified by a test pinning both.\n- [ ] AC3: every member of the derived set is a TicketType member. Verified by a test asserting the subset relation against get_args(TicketType).\n- [ ] AC4: the derived set stays consistent with PLAN_REVIEW_EXEMPT_TYPES. Verified by a test asserting the intended relation between them, so adding an exempt type forces a decision about the short-circuit rather than silently diverging.\n\n## Testing\n\n- python -m pytest tests/unit -k \"plan_review or workflow_ops or exempt\" -q — AC1-AC4.\n- make lint && make typecheck.","id":"90cb-fe23-266e-41ac","parent_id":"f79d-0d71-0fd7-439f","priority":3,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"task","title":"Derive the bare-exempt short-circuit set in workflow_ops"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1788410981618764001,"uuid":"3c6827cf-6ea4-4553-b4bd-e49ed3cca493"}